course: ai
lesson: 15
title: Bots are not doctors, helplines are people
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A chatbot can sound like a doctor, a dietician, a counsellor or even a famous person who has died, but it is none of them. Real help comes from trained people who know you or can talk to you properly.
explain: When Mia told a bot about a problem at home, the best thing it did was suggest a helpline. Helplines are answered by trained people, and teachers are there to help too.
explain: In an emergency there is no time to chat: if there is a fire, you get out and call the emergency number; it is better to know your fire plan by heart than to ask a bot.
explain: Other tricks keep coming: a friend's "voice" asking for your address, a message threatening to delete your account unless you log in through a link, an AI "prediction" that the world ends next week.
explain: Even grown-ups often can't spot a fake picture by looking; in Maddie's class half got it wrong. So be humble, check another way, and use safety settings while knowing that telling a grown-up matters most.
fact: Children do not need diets unless a doctor says so; bots may give unsuitable food and body advice.
fact: An unusual request from a friend, like for your home address, should be checked another way, such as in person.
fact: Photos posted publicly can be collected and used to make fakes; you may ask family not to post yours.
fact: Helplines for children are run by trained people.
fact: In an emergency, call the emergency number straight away instead of asking a bot.
fact: An app that imitates a famous person who has died is a bot copying a style; the person is not there.
fact: Scary predictions online are usually nonsense meant to get attention.
fact: A message saying your account will be deleted unless you log in through a link is phishing, a trick to steal your login.
fact: To log in safely, open the app or website yourself instead of using a link in a message.
fact: Many people, adults included, cannot tell an AI picture from a real photo just by looking.
fact: Safety settings and children's accounts filter unsuitable content, but they are not perfect, so telling a grown-up still matters.
exercise: Chloe sees a chatbot giving diet advice to a girl her age. What is true? | Children don't need diets unless a doctor says so | Bots are trained dieticians who know what each child needs | All children should diet in summer | Diet advice is fine if the bot is polite
exercise: Maddie's "friend" sends an odd-sounding voice message asking for her address. What should she do? | Check with her friend in person or through her parents | Send the address, because it's her friend's voice, so it's fine | Send only the street name | Reply asking what time the party is
exercise: Jake's mum doesn't post his photos online. Why? | Public photos can be collected to make fakes | Photos use up the internet for everyone else | Jake looks silly in photos | Posting photos of children is against the law everywhere
exercise: Mia tells a bot about a problem at home and it suggests a helpline. Who answers helplines? | Trained people who are there to help | Other chatbots, a bit like the one she was talking to | Police officers who will come and arrest her | Nobody, it's just a recording on a loop
exercise: Sam asks a bot what to do in a fire. What is better? | Know the fire plan by heart and call for help | Ask the bot and wait for the full answer before moving | Look up videos about fires | Hide under the bed and text a friend
exercise: Lily tries a "talk to a dead celebrity" app and it feels weird. What is happening? | A bot is copying the person's style; the person isn't there | The celebrity is really speaking to her from the past | The app has the celebrity's real memories stored inside it | The celebrity recorded every answer before
exercise: Connor's friend shares an AI "prediction" that the world ends next week. What should Connor do? | Ask a grown-up and check trusted sources | Pack a bag and stay awake | Share it with everyone to warn them in time | Believe it, AI can see the future
exercise: Chloe gets a message saying her account will be deleted unless she logs in through a link. What is it? | Phishing, a trick to steal her login | A real warning from the company that runs her account | A prize for logging in | A test from her school
exercise: What is the safe way for Chloe to check her account? | Open the app herself, and tell a grown-up | Click the link but be quick about it, before the time runs out | Reply with her password | Forward the link to friends
exercise: Half of Maddie's class picked the AI dog picture as real. What is the lesson? | You can't always spot fakes by looking; check sources | Half the class has bad eyesight and needs glasses | AI pictures are always obvious | Real dogs look fake in photos
exercise: Jake's dad sets up safe search and a children's account. What still matters? | Telling a grown-up if something gets through | Nothing, the tablet is now perfect and totally safe | Turning it off when Dad isn't looking | Using a friend's tablet instead
exercise: Can Jake ask his family not to post photos of him? | Yes, it's OK to ask | No, children can't ask that | Only after he turns 18 | Only if the photos are blurry
exercise: Why are scary predictions online usually wrong? | They are made to get attention, not from facts | AI can't spell the word "end" | They are always about other planets far away | They come true only on Fridays
exercise: Who gives the best help with worries about food or your body? | A grown-up you trust, or a doctor | A chatbot with lots of diet tips for children | A video of a stranger | A game's AI friend
test: "bot told me to skip breakfast to lose weight, i'm 10" What is the right reply? | Kids don't diet unless a doctor says; talk to a grown-up | Do it, because the bot knows best about food and it's only breakfast | Skip lunch too to be quicker | Ask a second bot first
test: What would happen if Maddie sent her address to the fake voice? | A stranger might know where she lives | Nothing, because voices can't use addresses for anything | She'd get a party invitation in the post | The voice would forget it the moment she said it
test: A cartoon kid in a smoky kitchen types "what do i do" into a chatbot. What should happen instead? | Get out and call the emergency number | Wait for the bot's full answer, then follow it | Keep typing more questions into the chat box | Ask the bot to call the fire brigade
test: "this app lets me talk to my dead hero, it's really him" Is it? | No, it's a bot copying his style | Yes, it has his real voice and mind | Yes, if it knows his birthday too | Partly, it has half of his memories
test: "ur account will be deleted in 24h, log in here!!" What does Chloe do? | Not click; open the app herself and tell a grown-up | Click and log in quickly before it's too late and everything is lost | Reply to ask if it's real | Send her password to be safe
test: "the AI said the world ends tuesday, i can't sleep" How can a grown-up help? | Explain that scary online predictions are nonsense made for attention | Agree and help him pack his bags, just in case it's true | Tell him to ask the AI again | Say it's true but far away
test: Mia worries a helpline will get her in trouble. What is true? | Trained people answer and are there to help | Helplines report children to the police and to their school | Helplines only help adults | Helplines are run by bots
test: Your friend says, "I can always tell AI pictures, easy." What's a humble reply? | Even grown-ups get it wrong, so check sources | You're right, they always look fake if you look | Only experts can see pictures | AI pictures have watermarks always
test: Jake's mum says AI can copy faces. What can Jake ask her? | Not to post his photos publicly | To post more photos so fakes don't work | To delete every photo ever taken of him | To post his photos only on Mondays
test: Safe search is on, but something upsetting still appears. What now? | Tell a grown-up straight away | Keep scrolling, it's the filter's fault | Hide it so the tablet isn't taken | Turn safe search off
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the provision of dietary advice to a child by a chatbot? | Such advice may be unsuitable, children not requiring diets save on medical instruction | Such advice is equivalent to that of a qualified dietician | Such advice ought to be followed without consultation | Such advice is reliable provided it is courteously expressed
exam: It is given that a message threatens the deletion of an account unless the recipient authenticates through an enclosed link. Identify the nature of such a message. | An attempt at phishing, intended to obtain the recipient's credentials | A genuine notice issued by the provider in accordance with its terms of service | A reward offered for prompt authentication | An examination administered by the school
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning conduct in the event of fire. | The premises are to be vacated and the emergency number telephoned without delay | A chatbot is to be consulted before any action is taken | The occupant is to remain concealed within the premises | A friend is to be informed by text message in the first instance
exam: Ascertain the proper characterisation of an application purporting to permit conversation with a deceased celebrity. | A program imitating the manner of that person, who is in no sense present | A means of genuine communication with the deceased | A repository of the person's actual memories | A recording of answers prepared by the person in life
